* {{l|en|guinea-pig}}, {{l|en|Guinea-pig}}
* {{l|en|Guinea pig}}

===Etymology===
The origin of "guinea" in "guinea pig" is uncertain. One theory is that the animals, which are originally from South America, were brought to Europe by way of [[Guinea]], leading people to think they had originated there.<ref name=wagner>{{cite-book|last=Wagner|first=Joseph E.|last2=Manning|first2=Patrick J|title=The Biology of the Guinea Pig|year=1976|publisher=Academic Press|isbn=0-12-730050-3}}</ref> "Guinea" was also frequently used in English to refer generally to any far-off, unknown country, and so the name may simply be a colorful reference to the animal's foreignness.<ref name=dictionary>{{cite-web|title=Guinea pig definition and meaning|publisher=Harper Collins|site=Collins Dictionary|url=https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/guinea-pig|accessdate=2023-08-31}}</ref> Others believe "guinea" may be an alteration of the word {{m|en|coney||rabbit}}; guinea pigs were referred to as "pig coneys" in Edward Topsell's 1607 treatise on quadrupeds.<ref name=wagner/>

{{rfe|en|Where did the "experimental subject" sense come from? Guinea pigs are not commonly used for that these days.}}

